  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nAs the stationary boundary that has been splayed out across our area\ncontinues to sag south as a cold front, showers and thunderstorms \nwill once again be possible. High probabilities for thunderstorm \nchances exist along and south of I-70, likely this afternoon and \nevening. The main threats will be damaging wind and locally heavy \nrainfall. Confidence in placement/timing of storms (or whether \nstorms actually fire off) is low. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nBy Sunday, large scale ridging builds into our west, heralding in \nnortherly flow and a broad area of surface high pressure. This \ntranslates to quieter and cooler conditions, with highs on Sunday and\nMonday only reaching the low 80s, while overnight lows fall into the\nlow 60s/upper 50s. Unfortunately, this reprieve is short lived as \nthe bulk of the ridge continues to push east into our area, bringing \na return temperatures in the low 90s by mid week. A disturbance to \nour north looks to bring the next chances for precipitation mid-week \nas it drags a cold front through the Great Lakes region, though we're\nstill a bit far out for details.",
